 "name": "How Do Wuhan Streets Feel? Six Perceptions (0–100)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants view 20 street views from central Wuhan one at a time and score each from 0 (lowest) to 100 (highest) on the paper's six perceptions: wealthy, safe, lively, beautiful, boring and depressing. The images are not the paper's stimuli (33 Baidu Street View forward views of central Wuhan, 2016–2022, cropped to remove the capture car and to the paper's 16:10 shape).\nOriginal study: Yang, Deng, Hu, Chao, Wan, Guan & Wei (2024), \"Urban perception by using eye movement data on street view images\", Transactions in GIS 28, 1021–1042. 2,040 Tencent street views of Wuhan's eight central districts were split into 34 groups of 60; 67 students, teachers, government employees and business people in Wuhan (34 men, 33 women, aged 18–60) each viewed 5–10 groups on an eye tracker while an assistant recorded their six 0–100 scores. Fixation features weighted the image segmentation, and a random forest mapped the six perceptions across the city.\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online and without eye tracking: participants set the scores on sliders themselves instead of saying them to an assistant; (2) 20 images per participant from 33 new Baidu images instead of 300–600 Tencent images; (3) only age group and gender are asked.",
